---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const html = '\n<p>Cash balance contributions are not published as a single IRS dollar cap. Three statutory ceilings do most of the work, and they move every year. The figures below are the ones the IRS actually announced. Anything that looks like “an owner age 55 can put in $X” on this site is an <strong>illustration</strong>, not a limit.</p>\n<h2>The three ceilings that matter</h2>\n<p>For 2026, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/401k-limit-increases-to-24500-for-2026-ira-limit-increases-to-7500">IR-2025-111</a> and <a href="https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/cola-increases-for-dollar-limitations-on-benefits-and-contributions">the IRS COLA table</a> set:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>401(k) elective deferral: $24,500.</strong> Catch-up is <strong>$8,000</strong> from age 50, and <strong>$11,250</strong> at ages 60 through 63.</li>\n<li><strong>Defined contribution annual additions: $72,000.</strong> That is the ceiling most SEP and profit-sharing designs feel. Compensation counted in the formula stops at <strong>$360,000</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Defined benefit annual benefit: $290,000.</strong> That is a retirement annuity ceiling, not a deposit. An Enrolled Actuary converts it into this year’s contribution. See the <a href="https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-defined-benefit-plan-benefit-limits">IRS benefit-limit page</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What those numbers were when the briefing started</h2>\n<p>In the fall of 2024 the working limits were a <strong>$23,000</strong> deferral, <strong>$69,000</strong> of defined-contribution additions, a <strong>$345,000</strong> compensation cap, and a <strong>$275,000</strong> defined-benefit dollar limit. On November 1, 2024, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/401k-limit-increases-to-23500-for-2025-ira-limit-remains-7000">IR-2024-285</a> raised the 2025 figures to a <strong>$23,500</strong> deferral, <strong>$70,000</strong> additions, a <strong>$350,000</strong> cap, and a <strong>$280,000</strong> benefit limit, and it introduced the higher catch-up at ages 60–63.</p>\n<p>The owner briefing walks those changes in the week they happened. Start with the <a href="/insights">archive</a>, then bring a census to a <a href="/contact">conversation</a> before anyone treats an illustration as a deduction.</p>\n';
---
<Base title="The limits that govern the plan, from 2024 through 2026. | Sterling Pension Group">
  <section class="wrap page">
    <p class="kicker">IRS limits</p>
    <h1>IRS retirement plan limits, 2024 through 2026.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Three IRS ceilings. No flat cash-balance cap. The briefing is dated so you can see which numbers were current.</p>
    <div class="guide" set:html={html} />
  </section>
</Base>
